{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785098630,"nonce":"4RRvxYJ+iQJ7KaxLJHPcxb7/v7k=","title":"Found while landing ADR-0061 s3-feed. **Latent before this slice, and the failure","message":"point moved rather than appeared** — so it is not a regression, but it is now\nreachable one step earlier and worth costing.\n\n`build_pod` gives a workspace Pod `fsGroup = WORKSPACE_GID (65532)` *unless* a\ndeclared sidecar service pins a non-root uid (ADR-0058 `run_as_user`), in which\ncase `service_fs_group` wins and the Pod's `fsGroup` becomes e.g. 999. The kubelet\nthen chowns the `/workspace` emptyDir to `root:999`, mode 0775 + setgid.\n\nThe fetcher runs as uid 65532 with `supplementalGroups: [65532]`, so against a\n`root:999 0775` directory it falls through to \"other\" (r-x) and **cannot write**.\nThe Step is refused its workspace before it starts.\n\nThe pre-existing shape of the same bug: the STEP also runs with\n`supplementalGroups: [65532]` and also is not in group 999, so it could not write\n`/workspace` either. The old `busybox` doorstop only escaped it because it ran as\n**root** (no securityContext at all), so `tar -xf -` over exec worked and only the\nstep failed, later and more confusingly. The unit test\n`workspace_steps_are_always_members_of_the_workspace_group` asserts the PodSpec\nshape and passes; it does not and cannot assert that the shape works.\n\nSo the combination \"a Step with `needs:` AND a sidecar service pinning a non-root\nuid\" is broken today, and has been. Options:\n\n1. Keep `fsGroup = WORKSPACE_GID` on workspace Pods and give the service its gid\n   via `supplementalGroups` instead. Inverts the d2301fb decision and two tests\n   assert the current behaviour, so it needs a deliberate call.\n2. Put BOTH gids in `supplementalGroups` and pick `fsGroup` by which volume matters\n   more — still only one volume gets chowned, so it does not actually fix it.\n3. Wait for ADR-0061 s2b: with a CSI volume, `fsGroupPolicy: None` means the\n   kubelet applies no fsGroup at all and **the driver owns the ownership contract**\n   (D2.5 spells this out, including creating `upperdir` as 65532:65532 mode 02775).\n   That removes the conflict rather than arbitrating it.\n\n(3) is probably the answer, which makes this a \"do not fix twice\" ticket rather\nthan an urgent one — but it must not be discovered again from a red dogfood run.\nNeeds a live test either way: this is precisely the \"reports success but\nstructurally cannot work\" class.","files":null}]}